  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "norwegian-folk-instruments-ensemble-voices-ii",
      "title": "Norwegian Folk Instruments — Ensemble Voices II",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** Nordic Folk | **Region:** nordic-norway | **Tier:** 3\n**Prerequisites:** regions-nordic-norway-02-instruments-voices-i\n**Tags:** nordic, norway, nordic, session, &"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "accordion, guitar\n\nNorwegian Folk Music belongs to the **Nordic Folk** curriculum. Annbjørg Lien\nKnut Buen\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **accordion, guitar** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-ireland",
      "title": "Compared with Ireland",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "This anchor unit establishes vocabulary for **Nordic Folk**. When you later study sibling nations in this block, compare each lesson to this chapter.\n\n**Optional Atlantic-trad link:** `regions-celtic-ireland-03-instruments-voices-ii` — useful if you completed the Celtic Ireland unit first."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "accordion, guitar\n\nStudy **norwegian folk music** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Music of Norway](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Norway) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **Nordic Folk**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **Hardanger fiddle, langeleik, voice**. Ensemble layer: **accordion, guitar**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **slått, pols, halling**. Dance context: **halling, springar**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **collectors, GRAPA**. Contemporary scenes: **concerts, festivals**. Tune repertoire anchor: **Fanitullen, Springar forms**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- accordion, guitar\n- Lead instruments: Hardanger fiddle, langeleik, voice\n- Ensemble: accordion, guitar\n- Forms: slått, pols, halling\n- Dance: halling, springar\n- History: collectors, GRAPA\n- Scenes: concerts, festivals\n- Repertoire: Fanitullen, Springar forms\n- Compared with Norway: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
      "title": "Key points",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Norwegian Folk Music is a living tradition with regional accent.\n- Focus: accordion, guitar.\n- Compared with Norway: shared heritage, distinct emphasis.\n- Forms: slått, pols, halling.\n- Dance: halling, springar.\n- Transmission: collectors, GRAPA."
        }
      ]
    },
